  "response": "The problem was posed in the only form nineteenth-century celestial mechanics allowed: a measured discrepancy between observation and prediction, which could be absorbed either by adding unseen matter to the solar system or by amending the law of attraction itself. Urbain Le Verrier announced in 1859 that Mercury's perihelion advanced faster than Newtonian theory predicted, and that announcement is by itself enough to rule out the claim that Le Verrier's original estimate of the residual precession was 38 arcseconds per year; given what he announced, no such estimate could have been his. Le Verrier had already, in 1846, successfully predicted the existence and position of Neptune from anomalies in the motion of Uranus, and that triumph led him to propose that an unseen intra-Mercurial planet or ring of matter caused Mercury's excess precession. The proposal of such a body resulted in the hypothetical intra-Mercurial planet being named Vulcan.\n\nLe Verrier's arcsecond-per-year reckoning of the anomaly stands in contrast to the figure established a generation later, when Simon Newcomb refined the unexplained residual to 43 arcseconds per century in 1882; that is, Mercury's perihelion advances 43 arcseconds per century beyond the Newtonian prediction. With the quantity thus fixed, the dispute could be settled only by asking what, if anything, in the inner solar system was capable of supplying it.\n\nAstronomers searched for Vulcan during total solar eclipses and in transits across the solar disk. Photographic plates obtained at the 1878 total solar eclipse confirmed Vulcan's orbital period as 19 days.\n\nThe decisive consideration, however, was not whether a faint body could be glimpsed near the Sun, but whether the dynamical bookkeeping of the inner solar system could tolerate one at all. Any mass sufficient to shift Mercury's perihelion by 43 arcseconds per century would have produced detectable perturbations in the orbit of Venus, and that constraint shows that no intra-Mercurial planet of the mass required to explain the residual precession exists. A nineteen-day orbital period fixed from eclipse photographs and the absence of any body heavy enough to account for the residual cannot both be true; the one leaves no room for the other. Either there is no such intra-Mercurial body, or a planet named Vulcan orbits the Sun inside the orbit of Mercury: exactly one of these two possibilities holds, and it cannot be both.\n\nThe second horn of the original question was answered from theory rather than from the telescope. Albert Einstein derived a perihelion advance of 43 arcseconds per century from his field equations in November 1915, and no adjustable parameter entered that calculation, the figure following from the structure of the field alone. That derivation confirms that the perihelion residual arose from a deficiency in Newtonian gravitation rather than from an undiscovered planet, and this is the conclusion the physics community ultimately drew.",
